<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · religion</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jainism</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Jain religion</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Jainism is an Indian religion that teaches liberation of the soul through non-violence, truthfulness, non-attachment and strict self-discipline. Its followers revere 24 tirthankaras, the last of whom, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahavira">Mahavira</a>, lived in the 6th or 5th century BCE. Divided into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/digambara">Digambara</a> and Shvetambara traditions, Jains have long been prominent as merchants and patrons of temples, such as those of Palitana.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jainism.jpg" alt="Jainism" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Matthew Logelin · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Shravanbelgola_Gomateshvara_feet_prayer1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
